# The interfaces that have a C++ class in the Runtime: mitto::<name>, declared
# in native/dom/<file>.h. The bindings generator writes bindings for these, and
# only these are exposed to a page until every interface has bindings (roadmap,
# M7). docs/runtime.md says how to add one.
#
# A member whose behaviour the Runtime does not have yet is a Known Gap. Its
# binding is installed as Chrome installs it, and throws when a page calls it.
# An entry names its Known Gaps one of two ways:
#
# - `missing` names them, and the class has every other member.
# - `implemented` names the members the class has, and every other member is a
#   Known Gap. "get x" names the getter of attribute x alone. The generator
#   writes a reflected attribute of a string or a boolean itself, so it needs
#   no name. "indexed getter" and "named getter" name those operations. An
#   indexed or named property operation that is a Known Gap is not installed
#   at all.
#
# An event handler attribute that the class lacks is not a Known Gap: the
# bindings give it Chrome's behaviour themselves.
#
# `made_by_known_gaps = true` says that a Known Gap may make an object of the
# class with nothing given, which the class's static CreateForKnownGap() makes,
# for a Known Gap that gives one, and for one that gives an object of an
# interface without a class that inherits from it. Then `constructor` names
# the constructor as any member, and a constructor that is a Known Gap makes
# the object that way. Without it, the class has its constructor.
#
# "maplike" or "setlike" in `implemented` gives a read-only declaration of
# that kind its behaviour: the bindings write its members, which read the
# class's MaplikeEntries() or SetlikeValues().
